    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian cultural life brings shared languages, celebrations, and social practices into contact with substantial regional variation. Food, music, craftsmanship, and everyday etiquette provide concrete ways to understand that diversity without treating all Iranians as culturally identical.",
    "tags": ["Iranian culture", "Languages", "Social customs", "Regional traditions", "Food", "Living heritage"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:46:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:46:26Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "People, Values and Culture in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "An introduction to Iran’s languages, social customs, celebrations, regional food, musical traditions, and craft knowledge."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "shared-and-local",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Shared references, many ways of belonging",
        "content": "Culture becomes easier to understand when broad labels are connected to particular people, places, and activities. In Iran, language, regional affiliation, and wider cultural belonging intersect rather than forming a single uniform identity. Multilingualism is important in many regions, and the language someone uses at home does not by itself explain every other aspect of their cultural life. [s1]\n\nThis guide approaches culture through those intersections. Its branches distinguish identity from language, social customs from celebrations, and household practices from regional traditions. Food, clothing, crafts, oral expression, and contemporary life are related subjects, but each asks different questions: who makes something, who teaches it, where it is practiced, and how its meaning changes. The documented examples below offer starting points rather than a checklist defining an authentic Iranian person. [s1][s4][s6][s7]"
      ,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s4", "s6", "s7"]
      },
      {
        "id": "languages",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Language is a map with overlapping boundaries",
        "content": "Persian, also called Farsi, belongs to the Iranian branch of the Indo-European language family. That branch also includes Kurdish, Balochi, Gilaki, Mazandarani, and other languages and varieties. “Iranian language” is consequently a technical linguistic term with a geographical reach beyond Iran’s borders. It does not mean that every language spoken in the country belongs to that family. [s2]\n\nAzerbaijani and other Turkic languages, Arabic, Armenian, and Neo-Aramaic are among the languages documented in Iran. Their histories include settlement, trade, religious communities, and interaction with neighboring regions. For an international reader, this makes a distinction between “Persian” and “Iranian” essential: Persian is central to the country’s literary history, while Iranian cultural life encompasses additional linguistic traditions. Language contact also means that vocabulary and everyday usage cannot always be understood through isolated language categories. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "social-customs",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Courtesy is an interaction",
        "content": "Taarof is a useful introduction to Iranian social customs because it connects words with gestures. Anthropologist William O. Beeman describes it as a system of ritualized deference and status negotiation. Examples include inviting someone to pass through a doorway first, offering an honored seat, and using language that elevates the listener while modestly lowering the speaker. [s3]\n\nOffers and refusals can both participate in this convention. That does not make every offer insincere: the meaning depends on the relationship and the unfolding exchange. Hospitality, visiting, and gift-giving therefore require attention to context rather than a fixed rule about how many times someone must refuse. Beeman’s account also documents comparable practices across different religious and linguistic communities within Iran. Courtesy provides a shared social vocabulary, while particular interactions retain room for affection, obligation, humor, and disagreement. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "celebrations",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Seasonal celebrations connect households and wider regions",
        "content": "Nowruz marks the arrival of spring and is celebrated across a broad region that includes Iran and several neighboring societies. UNESCO presents it as a shared cultural practice associated with renewal, social connection, and the transmission of traditions. Its multiple names and participating communities are reminders that a celebration may be deeply meaningful in Iran without belonging exclusively to Iran. [s4]\n\nFor readers exploring celebrations, the useful question is how a broad seasonal occasion becomes a particular family or community event. A heritage description identifies recognized practices, but it cannot establish what every household does or how strongly every individual participates. The celebrations branch should therefore be read alongside regional culture and family life: the calendar supplies a common reference, while people give an occasion its specific form. This distinction also prevents international recognition from becoming a claim that a living tradition has one permanently fixed version. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "regional-food",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Regional food makes diversity tangible",
        "content": "Gilan, along the southwestern Caspian coast, offers a particularly clear example of regional cuisine. Christian Bromberger’s account emphasizes rice, garden vegetables, kitchen herbs, eggs, fish, and sour flavors. Mirza qasemi combines eggplant, garlic, tomato, and egg; kate is a rice preparation; and pickled garlic belongs to the region’s repertoire of sharp accompaniments. [s5]\n\nThese details explain more than a generic list of national dishes. They connect cooking with local agriculture, available ingredients, and learned preferences. The same account also records changing eating habits, including the spread of bread consumption and the influence of urban dietary models. Regional cuisine is consequently both a marker of distinction and a field of change. Food and cooking deserve their own branch because a dish can illuminate environment, household knowledge, and local identity at the same time. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "music-and-crafts",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Culture is also knowledge learned through practice",
        "content": "The radif is a repertoire underlying Iranian classical music. Musicians learn organized melodic material and use that knowledge in performance and improvisation. UNESCO’s description emphasizes oral instruction between teacher and student, showing that preservation involves trained memory and interpretation as well as written or recorded material. [s6]\n\nCarpet weaving offers a parallel example of embodied knowledge. UNESCO’s Kashan account describes designing, preparing materials, knotting, dyeing, and other specialized tasks learned through apprenticeship. A finished carpet therefore represents a network of skills rather than an isolated decorative object. These examples connect clothing and everyday crafts with oral traditions: knowledge can travel through demonstration, repetition, and correction. [s7]\n\nTransmission also depends on livelihoods. Reporting from Kashan in 2024 documented unsold carpets, obstacles to international transactions, and pressure on weavers’ earnings. Such evidence complicates a purely celebratory heritage narrative: recognition and practical economic security are different matters. [s8]",
        "source_ids": ["s6", "s7", "s8"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-living-culture",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading culture as a living subject",
        "content": "The most productive route through these subjects is to move between a shared practice and a specific setting. Follow a language into a region, a meal into a household, or a craft into the circumstances of its makers. The examples here show why continuity and change should be investigated together: a tradition survives through people continuing to use, teach, reinterpret, and earn a living from it. [s1][s5][s6][s7][s8]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s5", "s6", "s7", "s8"]
      }
    ],
