  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-2-1",
    "page_path": "/explore/culture/iranian-identity/",
    "name": "Iranian Identity",
    "level": 2,
    "parent_path": "/explore/culture/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iranian identity combines historical memory, territorial belonging, cultural traditions, and modern ideas of nationhood. Its meanings have changed over time and coexist with local, linguistic, religious, and other affiliations.",
    "tags": ["Iranian identity", "Historical memory", "Nationhood", "Cultural belonging", "Local identities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T07:46:26Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T07:46:26Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iranian Identity: History, Belonging and Interpretation | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian identity through changing historical meanings, cultural memory, modern nationhood, and the relationship between national and local belonging."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meanings-of-belonging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Several meanings within one phrase",
        "content": "Iranian identity can describe belonging to a country, attachment to a historical homeland, or participation in cultural traditions. These meanings overlap, but they are not interchangeable. Encyclopaedia Iranica’s overview describes a collective identification shaped by historical experience and cultural memory, while also recognizing kinship, language, religion, locality, and region as other sources of allegiance. A person need not have only one of these attachments. [s1]\n\nThis makes identity a subject for historical explanation rather than a test of authenticity. Shared memories can connect people whose daily languages or family histories differ. Equally, a common national label does not establish agreement about the past or about which traditions matter most. The task is to identify the particular meaning of belonging in a particular setting. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Continuity is a question to investigate",
        "content": "Scholars disagree about how modern nations relate to older communities. Ahmad Ashraf distinguishes approaches that treat nations as ancient and enduring, approaches emphasizing their modern construction, and a historical approach that recognizes older attachments without projecting modern citizenship backward. The disagreement concerns interpretation, not simply the existence of old texts or names. [s2]\n\nA reference to Iran in a medieval work may demonstrate historical consciousness, but it does not prove that its author understood nationality in the same way as a citizen today. Conversely, modern nation-building did not have to begin with an empty cultural landscape. Older stories, territorial names, and literary traditions supplied material that later writers could select and reinterpret. Holding these distinctions together avoids both an unchanging national essence and an assumption that the historical past was irrelevant. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sasanian-political-idea",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The Sasanian political vocabulary",
        "content": "Historian Gherardo Gnoli locates a decisive political formulation of Iran in the Sasanian period, beginning in the third century CE. His account distinguishes earlier ethnic, linguistic, and religious uses of related terms from the Sasanian concept of Eranshahr, a kingdom of the Iranians. Royal authority and Zoroastrian religious institutions helped give that political idea its form. [s3]\n\nThis is an argument about the development of a political concept, not a claim that modern borders or citizenship already existed. It also shows why the history of the name Iran deserves separate attention: the survival of a word and changes in the political community it describes must be studied together. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "medieval-reconstruction",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cultural reconstruction after imperial change",
        "content": "The fall of the Sasanian state did not leave Iranian identity either wholly intact or wholly erased. Ashraf’s account of the medieval period describes literati reconstructing a cultural idea of Iran within Islamic society. The development of New Persian as a literary language and renewed engagement with traditional history were important parts of that process. [s4]\n\nThe distinction between political rule and cultural expression matters here. Literature could preserve, reshape, and circulate an understanding of Iran across changing dynasties. Its significance cannot be reduced to whether a single state controlled the same territory throughout the period. Nor should cultural reconstruction be mistaken for the modern civic identity of equal members of a nation-state; the source explicitly distinguishes those concepts. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "modern-nationhood",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Modern nationhood changed the terms of debate",
        "content": "During the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, ideas of nation, citizenship, and popular political belonging altered older understandings of identity. Ashraf traces how intellectuals adapted inherited cultural material to new political arguments. Constitutional nationalism, Pahlavi state nationalism, and later popular nationalist movements represented different projects rather than a single uncontested expression of national feeling. [s5]\n\nLanguage itself registered change. The Persian term mellat, previously associated with a religious community, acquired a modern national meaning. Such shifts show that identity involves changing concepts as well as inherited attachments. A historical account should therefore ask who was speaking, which audience they addressed, and whether loyalty was being directed toward a dynasty, a religious community, a territory, or the nation. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-symbols-and-locality",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to explore the subject",
        "content": "The branches below separate questions that are often collapsed. The idea of identity concerns definitions and interpretations; the name Iran concerns terminology; national symbols and historical flags concern visible expressions of belonging; and national and local identities concern overlapping attachments. None supplies a complete explanation on its own. Read together, they provide a way to understand historical continuity while keeping change and internal diversity visible. [s1][s2][s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s5"]
      }
